    {
      "actual_eos_at_stop": true,
      "answer_segment": "To determine how many of Farmer Brown's animals are chickens, we can set up a system of equations based on the information given. Let's define the variables first:\n\n- Let \\( c \\) be the number of chickens.\n- Let \\( k \\) be the number of cows.\n\nFrom the problem, we know two things:\n1. The total number of animals is 20.\n2. The total number of legs is 70.\n\nWe can express these two pieces of information as the following equations:\n1. \\( c + k = 20 \\)\n2. \\( 2c + 4k = 70 \\) (since chickens have 2 legs each and cows have 4 legs each)\n\nWe can simplify the second equation by dividing every term by 2:\n\\[ c + 2k = 35 \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( c + k = 20 \\)\n2. \\( c + 2k = 35 \\)\n\nTo eliminate \\( c \\), we can subtract the first equation from the second:\n\\[ (c + 2k) - (c + k) = 35 - 20 \\]\n\\[ c + 2k - c - k = 15 \\]\n\\[ k = 15 \\]\n\nSo, there are 15 cows. Now we substitute \\( k = 15 \\) back into the first equation to find \\( c \\):\n\\[ c + 15 = 20 \\]\n\\[ c = 20 - 15 \\]\n\\[ c = 5 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of chickens is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
